#!/opt/conda/bin/python
"""rs-memory — a research agent's memory, kept as plain files the PI can read.

Everything lives under /workspace/memory/ (RS_MEMORY_DIR overrides it):

  front.md                      the front page: line of inquiry, beliefs, in
                                flight, open threads; one item per line, each
                                with an id; its number in the header; then
                                "What we know about the data", the current
                                accepted facts, written from the record at
                                every write of the page and never read back
  front/<n>.md                  every earlier front page, never deleted
  journal/<yyyy-mm>/J-<n>.md    one entry per unit of work
  staged/F-<n>.md, Q-, R-, D-   claims waiting for the PI
  record/F-<n>.md, Q-, R-, D-   accepted findings, questions, retractions,
                                facts about the data
  struck/                       staged claims the PI struck
  skills/candidates/earned-*/   proposed skills, loaded by no agent
  skills/active/earned-*/       promoted skills, copied into the agents' homes
  skills.log                    one line per skill event
  consolidated                  the last journal entry a consolidation covered
  runs/                         consolidation runs: brief, delta, log
  checks/<id>/<n>/              a finding's checks, in a researcher-subagents
                                project only: a re-run (rerun/, rerun.md) or a
                                referee run (snapshot/, verdict.md); never
                                deleted, the audit trail
  sessions/<id>.json            a session's own switches (`switch`), in a
                                researcher-subagents project, and (planner
                                on) what the session holds; always in the
                                top memory
  planning.json                 (planner on) which session holds the
                                project's top, the planning session
  jobs/job-<n>.md               a long run the agent left going (its pid, its
                                command line, its log); marked done, never
                                deleted

That folder is the agent's own. Beside it, in the workspace, rs-memory writes
the pages the PI reads, views written from it (the store stays the single
source of truth):

  README.md                     the PI's front page: what waits for them (each
                                staged claim with its checks and the words to
                                decide it), what is running, where things
                                stand, what is known
  staging/<id>.md               one page per staged claim
  results/<id>/<id>.md          one page per accepted entry, with how it stands
                                now
  results/<id>/evidence/        a read-only copy of its evidence, made at accept
                                and never rewritten
  results/index.md              everything accepted, by topic, with its standing
  logbook/<date>-<topic>.md     the logbook: the story of the work, one entry
                                per topic, filed by `log` from the agent's
                                draft, read-only, never rewritten or removed

Every page is rebuilt after each verb that changes what it shows, and at open
(how an existing project gets its pages). Each starts with rs-memory's marker
line; a file without it is never overwritten or removed, and is reported until
the PI moves it. A page that cannot be written never fails the verb. The
logbook's entries are not pages: each is written once, when it is filed. The
logbook tells the story; the record holds the truth: where they disagree, an
entry's standing in the record is what holds.

Verbs:
  open [--hook] [--agent claude|pi]  what to read at the start of a session
  note HEADLINE --kind K [--body TEXT]
  checkpoint ITEM...            replace the "In flight" part of the front page
  claim STATEMENT --title T --scope S --evidence FILE... [--hedge H] [relations]
        [--topic T] [--rerun CMD | --no-rerun REASON | --question | --fact]
  amend ID [--statement S] [--title T] [--scope S] [--hedge H] [--evidence FILE...]
        [relations] [--topic T] [--rerun CMD | --no-rerun REASON]
                                correct a staged claim in place
  rerun-command ID (--rerun CMD | --no-rerun REASON)
  rerun ID  /  referee ID [--agent claude|pi]
  check ID... [--agent claude|pi]  both checks of several findings, in parallel
  switch [--subagents on|off] [--checks off|staged|all]
                                this session's switches: shown, or switched for
                                this session only
  accept ID... [--over-objection]  /  strike ID... --reason R
  view [ID | --topic T]         the state of knowledge, computed at every call
  recall WORD... [--body]       the journal, the claims and the logbook
  log --topic SLUG --file DRAFT file a logbook entry from the agent's draft
  skill propose|used|promote|retire|sync|list
  consolidate --prompt [--out FILE]  /  consolidate --apply DELTA
  close [--hook] [--agent claude|pi]  end of session (--hook: journal it only)
  analyst-check [--hook claude]  whether an analyst's reply names a results
                                folder holding its writeup.md (reads only)
  job add --pid N --log PATH --name TEXT [--expect-end TEXT]
  job list [--all]  /  job done ID
  plan [--agent claude|pi]      (planner on) take the project's top: this
                                session plans
  take PACKAGE [--agent claude|pi]  (planner on) take a package: this
                                session works in it
  release                       give back what this session holds
  package add NAME --charter FILE  /  package charter NAME --charter FILE
  package done NAME  /  package close NAME  /  package list [--json]

Nothing rs-memory starts outlives the call that started it. `close` runs
the consolidation (a headless agent on a brief, which merges its delta with
consolidate --apply) and waits for it, then says whether the front page was
rewritten; its agent is stopped after CONSOLIDATION_LIMIT_S seconds.
`close --hook` (the session's end hook) only journals the end, fast; the
next `open` then consolidates first, when the journal has a session's end
after the last consolidation, and its text says how that went. `rerun` and
`referee` wait for their check and print its result; `check` starts the
checks of several findings side by side and waits for them all. Each run
stays in its caller's process group and the kernel stops it when its caller ends; a
caller stopped with SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP stops its run first. What a
run starts (the re-run's command, an agent) runs under a keeper that leads
a process group of its own and takes that whole group down when its run
ends, however it ends (a SIGKILL to the caller's process group included),
so nothing the command left in the background keeps going. A check whose
run was stopped reads failed.

The checks (--rerun, rerun-command, rerun, referee, --over-objection and
accept's conditions on a finding) exist only in a project whose record,
/workspace/.orchestrator/project.json, says "subagents": true; anywhere else
every verb behaves as it always has.

Such a project has two switches, what a new session starts with: the
record's "research_switches" object, {"subagents": "on"|"off", "checks":
"off"|"staged"|"all"} (a record without it, or without a key, reads as
subagents on, checks staged). `switch` records a running session's own
switches, keyed by its id (CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, PI_SESSION_ID); they win
over the record in that session only. The checks switch decides accept: with
"staged" or "all" a finding needs both checks, as always; with "off" it can
be accepted without them (a finding needs no --rerun either), and its record
entry names what it lacked (`unchecked`), its pages say "not checked", as do
the pages of a finding staged with the checks off (`checks: off`) until a
check runs on it. The checks' verbs work whatever the switch. "all" differs
from "staged" only in the instructions. `open` says which switches hold.

The record's object may also hold "planner": "off"|"on" (absent = off), a
project setting only: no session switches it. With the planner off, every
session works in the top memory, RS_MEMORY_DIR (default /workspace/memory),
as above. With it on, the work is split into work packages, each a folder
<workspace>/packages/<name>/ (a name like a project's: letters, digits, '-'
and '_', a letter or digit first) laid out like the workspace's top: its
front page README.md, staging/, results/, logbook/, analyses/ and its own
memory/, plus charter.md (its questions, its data, what is out of its scope;
written by `package add` and `package charter` from a draft with the
sections "## Questions", "## Data" and "## Out of scope") and package.json,
its status: {"name", "state": "open"|"done"|"closed", "created", "changed",
"holder": null | {"session", "pid", "start", "since"}}. A session holds the
top (`plan`: the planning session, one at a time), one package (`take`), or
nothing; what it holds is in its file under the top memory's sessions/, and
the holder in package.json or the top memory's planning.json. Taking records
the session's id, its agent's process id and that process's start time
(/proc/<pid>/stat), so a reused pid never reads as the holder; a take is
refused while the recorded holder's process is alive with that start time,
and a holder whose process has ended reads as free. The agent's process is
CLAUDE_PID under Claude Code; under pi it is the first process above this
command whose environment does not carry the same PI_SESSION_ID (pi's bash
tool sets it for the commands it runs; pi itself does not have it). A
session that holds a package works in that package's memory; the planning
session, and every session's reads while it holds nothing, in the top; a
session that holds nothing is refused every verb that writes. The record,
the referee's model and a re-run's folder are the workspace's in a package
too. RS_MEMORY_DIR naming a package's memory is refused: a package's memory
is reached by taking the package. Taking a memory first consolidates its
last session when that one ended without `close` (as `open` does without
the planner); `open` of a session that holds nothing lists the packages and
consolidates nothing; `close` and `close --hook` act on the memory the
session holds, then give it back. Only the planning session adds a package,
changes a charter (journalled in the package as an entry naming the
sections that changed, so its session sees it at its next `open`) or
closes a package; the package's own session or the planning session marks
it done. A done or closed package can no longer be taken; a closed one takes
no writes. The top's front page lists the packages, each linked to its own.
Lock order: a writer holding a package's lock may take the top's; one
holding the top's never takes a package's.

`package list --json` prints one object for RS and the browser; its shape
is PACKAGES_JSON_DOC, which `rs-memory package list --help` prints.

A project whose record says "companion": true keeps a companion's memory:
front.md's parts are "Standing preferences" (P-<n>, kept by the consolidation
from the PI's own words), "In flight" and "Open threads", with no line of
inquiry and no beliefs; the consolidation proposes no claims; open leaves out
the knowledge and staged-claims blocks while they are empty; and README.md
shows what waits for the PI only while a claim is staged, then what is
running, the tasks (one line per folder under analyses/, linked to its
README.md), where things stand, the logbook, and what is known only once
something is accepted. Every verb works as it does in a researcher. A record
that says both "companion": true and "subagents": true is refused by every
verb.

A fact (D-<n>, claim --fact) is a descriptive result about the data: an
inventory line, a gap, a count. It needs a scope and evidence like a finding,
but never the checks, in any project: the PI's accept is all it takes. Its
standing is computed like a finding's.

It never deletes anything under the memory folder: a struck claim moves to
struck/, an old front page to front/, an amended claim keeps every earlier
version in its body, a job marked done keeps its file. Ids and the front page
are written under one lock file, so two sessions never hand out the same
number.

A staged claim may be amended (accepted entries are never edited: a correction
of one is a new claim that supersedes it). Its checks are bound to the hashes
of its statement, scope, hedge and evidence, so amending any of these voids
them by construction; a topic or a relation does not.

A job's state is computed at every call, never stored:
  running   its pid exists and /proc/<pid>/cmdline is byte for byte the
            command line read when it was registered (a reused pid reads as
            not running);
  ended     not running, and its log is a regular file that can be read; RS
            never saw the exit code, so this says only "the process is gone
            and its log is there", which a crash also is: the log's last line
            is shown with it, in open too, so the reader can tell;
  gone      not running, and its log is missing or not a readable file;
  done      marked with job done (whatever the process does).

Standard library only; run by RS's own interpreter.
"""
